# Proposed by the swarm (2026-10-15, board 0.22); the documenter may edit or drop it.
# Story: a laptop beside a larger monitor. A docked window is sent across with the key. It fills its place there: not four fifths of it.
# Right after a window has been taken to another display, macOS still judges what it may grow to by the display it came from: asked to be
# as high as its new place, it stops at the foot of the laptop's display. Arbor took that for an app that will not grow, centred the window in
# its place and remembered the short size, and so it lay until it next went out of view.
# That is the screen's answer and not the window's: nothing is made of it, and the window is asked again when macOS has caught up.
# Seen on a hosted Mac, from 1280x720 to 1920x1080. Here the laptop's display is 1440x900.
quiet
displays 2
open Safari
open Notes
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
wait 3
focus 102
move-to-display next
expect ok
# The journey: at its place's corner at once, as high as the laptop's display let it be (its foot at 900). Never in the middle of its place.
expect at 102 1452 12
expect size 102 942x888
wait 1.1
# Whole within about a second, in one further change, and still at the corner.
expect at 102 1452 12
expect size 102 942x1056
wait 10
expect still
expect size 102 942x1056
# Nothing was remembered: there and back again, it is as high as its place.
move-to-display next
wait 3
move-to-display next
wait 3
expect at 102 1452 12
expect size 102 942x1056
# The way to the laptop's display is one move, and nothing waits.
move-to-display next
expect moved-once
wait 5
expect still
